ury operations have all  reacted very strongly very a  in a concerted way in order to make sure  that they'd be there would be liquidity  available that the banks could function  and markets reaction was actually  relatively moderate I mean it markets  went down and then up a little bit so  it's down a you know by about three  percent if I recall the lira which is  the currency of Turkey went down as well  a little bit but not by much and in the  main there was an orderly functioning of  the markets after something that was  massively disorderly of course April of  2013 you travel to New York at the  Economic Club of New York you gave a  most important speech and you talked  about the different speeds of Nations  you talked about America is a two-speed  nation and the third speed nation the  ones most struggling were Europe and  Japan you were right it's gotten worse  within a broad pan-european perspective  how do you judge the immediacy of  actions that need to be taken across a  wide set of issues you know if anything  in Europe the decision made by the  British people to exist the European  Union has accelerated in our view the  need to consolidate the European Union  to give a strong message to the European  people about the benefit that Europe can  actually deliver for them concentrating  on the positive focusing on key issues  for the people of Europe in is in our  view an immediate and urgent response  that needs to bring together monetary  policy fiscal space wherever it is  available and those reforms that will  actually deliver value for people it's  it's all very well to say or we need  consolidation here and we need reforms  there what reforms what benefits what  what value will it bring to the European  people so that they understand that  there is value in being together  David folkert slander out Deutsche Bank  is pushing aggressively for a  substantial bailout program of the  Italian banks Bloomberg News had an  article today on the Bundesbank  begi